---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";

/* Sourced from docs/notes/competitor-facts-2026-08-15.md, sections 3 and H,
 * and from nothing else.
 *
 * THE OVERCLAIM THAT IS BANNED HERE, same as on /vs/aitum-multistream:
 * obs-multi-rtmp DOES send different audio to different destinations. It selects
 * an OBS mixer track per output; the summing happened upstream in OBS. Writing
 * that it cannot is false.
 *
 * WHY THIS PAGE IS NOT THE AITUM PAGE WITH THE NAMES SWAPPED. The two plugins
 * have the same audio model, so if both pages argued about the mixing desk they
 * would be one page published twice -- thin to a crawler and duplicated to
 * SonarCloud. They differ elsewhere, and the difference is worth a page of its
 * own: obs-multi-rtmp's `Get from OBS` shares ONE encode across every
 * destination, which removes the CPU objection almost entirely and leaves the
 * uplink standing alone. So this page argues topology -- how many copies of the
 * broadcast leave the building -- and the Aitum page argues the mixing desk.
 *
 * The star count that used to appear in this project's copy is deliberately
 * absent: it is not in the facts file, and a number nobody re-checks is the kind
 * of claim this page cannot afford.
 */

/* Header cell one is "What leaves the stream PC", NOT "Capability".
 * scripts/check-build.mjs treats any table headed "Capability" as a copy of the
 * canonical comparison table and requires every row of it to appear verbatim in
 * docs/COMPARISON.md. This table is not that table and should not pretend to be
 * -- it describes one machine's outbound load, which COMPARISON.md does not
 * cover. */
const outbound = [
  [
    "Video encodes running on the stream PC",
    "One, shared, with Get from OBS — or one per destination when you want different bitrates",
    "None. The picture is stream-copied through untouched",
  ],
  [
    "Outbound streams from your connection",
    "One per destination",
    "One, to the server, regardless of how many destinations there are",
  ],
  [
    "Audio encodes on the stream PC",
    "One per destination",
    "None. The mixing and the audio encode both happen on the server",
  ],
  [
    "Where a mix gets summed",
    "OBS's own audio mixer, upstream of the plugin",
    "The server, per destination, with a gain per channel-to-channel cell",
  ],
];

const merits = [
  {
    h: "Actively maintained",
    p: "Developed by sorayuki, at v0.7.4.3 when this was checked, with regular compatibility releases tracking OBS Studio 30.x, 31.x and 32.x. In a plugin ecosystem where the usual failure is silent abandonment two OBS majors ago, that is the feature that matters most.",
  },
  {
    h: "Get from OBS is genuinely good engineering",
    p: "Sharing the main OBS encoder across every destination means fanning out to four platforms costs one encode, not four. This is the same idea as a shared rendition on a server, implemented locally, and it is the reason the CPU argument against this plugin is mostly wrong.",
  },
  {
    h: "Independent re-encoding when you want it",
    p: "Separate encoder instances per destination, with their own bitrates and resolutions, for the platform that will not take your source.",
  },
  {
    h: "Free, GPL-2.0, and inside OBS already",
    p: "No cost, no account, no server, no second machine. It ingests whatever OBS ingests and publishes over RTMP and RTMPS.",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="obs-multi-rtmp vs polyemesis: multistream from OBS"
  description="The obs-multi-rtmp plugin adds one RTMP output per destination inside OBS. polyemesis fans out from a server. Both free — what changes is your uplink."
  path="/vs/obs-multi-rtmp"
>
  <Breadcrumbs
    crumbs={[
      { label: "polyemesis", href: "/" },
      { label: "Comparison", href: "/comparison" },
      { label: "obs-multi-rtmp", },
    ]}
  />

  <section class="wrap py-12 sm:py-16">
    <p class="eyebrow">Compared</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-balance text-4xl font-bold leading-tight sm:text-5xl">
      obs-multi-rtmp uploads once per platform.
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-pretty text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted">
      The obs-multi-rtmp plugin adds a second, third and fourth RTMP output
      inside OBS, and each one is a separate upload from the machine you are
      broadcasting on. That is the whole shape of it, and for most people it is
      fine. This page is about the point where it stops being fine, which is
      your connection rather than your processor.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-8 max-w-3xl rounded-token border-l-2 border-primary bg-surface/60 py-4 pl-5 pr-4">
      <p class="text-pretty leading-relaxed text-fg">
        <strong>Give the plugin its due first:</strong> its
        <span class="font-mono text-[13px]">Get from OBS</span> option shares one
        encoder across every destination, so multi-RTMP in OBS does
        <em>not</em> cost you an encode per platform. The processor argument
        against it is mostly wrong. What it cannot share is the uplink — four
        destinations is the same bytes sent four times.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* -------------------------------------------------------- the topology -- */}
  <section class="border-t border-line py-16" data-reveal>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-medium">What actually leaves the stream PC</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        The plugin runs inside OBS, so every destination it adds is another
        output from that machine. polyemesis is a server, so the machine sends
        one stream and the fan-out happens somewhere with a better connection
        than a house.
      </p>
      <div class="scroll-hint mt-6 max-w-5xl overflow-x-auto rounded-token border border-line">
        <table class="cmp w-full min-w-[46rem] border-collapse text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-surface">
            <tr class="border-b border-line">
              <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-medium">What leaves the stream PC</th>
              <th scope="col" class="w-64 px-4 py-3 text-left font-medium">obs-multi-rtmp</th>
              <th scope="col" class="w-64 px-4 py-3 text-left font-medium text-primary">polyemesis</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              outbound.map(([what, plugin, poly]) => (
                <tr class="border-b border-line/60 last:border-0">
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-fg">{what}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-muted">{plugin}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-muted">{poly}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Read the second row on a domestic upload. A 6 Mbps broadcast to four
        platforms is 24 Mbps sustained out of a connection that is also carrying
        the game, and every platform is receiving an identical copy. Moving the
        fan-out onto a server replaces that with a single 6 Mbps send — which is
        the argument for a server, and it is a bandwidth argument, not an audio
        one.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-subtle">
        It cuts the other way too, and belongs on the page: the server has to
        live somewhere, be patched, and be paid for. obs-multi-rtmp has no such
        bill and never goes down separately from OBS.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ------------------------------------------------------------ the audio -- */}
  <section class="border-t border-line bg-surface/30 py-16" data-reveal>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-medium">On the audio, precisely</h2>
      <div class="mt-6 grid max-w-5xl gap-5 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="card p-6">
          <p class="font-medium text-fg">What it does</p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-pretty text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
            Each output is assigned one of OBS's audio tracks — mixer IDs 1
            through 6 — and that track is what the destination receives. So
            different platforms genuinely can get different audio, and any
            comparison telling you otherwise is wrong about this plugin.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card p-6">
          <p class="font-medium text-fg">Where the mix came from</p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-pretty text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
            The plugin does not sum anything. Tracks 1 to 6 were summed by OBS's
            own mixer before the plugin saw them, so what you are choosing per
            destination is which of six pre-built mixes to send. polyemesis
            builds the mix per destination instead, from the tracks as they
            arrived.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-subtle">
        Six tracks is a real ceiling and also a generous one — most broadcasts
        need two. The distinction earns a page because of what it costs to
        change: a new mix in OBS is a new track layout, and it is not something
        to be editing while live.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ---------------------------------------------------------- their merits -- */}
  <section class="border-t border-line py-16" data-reveal>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-medium">What obs-multi-rtmp does well</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Checked against the project's own repository and releases on
        <span class="font-mono text-[13px] text-fg">15 August 2026</span>.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-5 sm:grid-cols-2">
        {
          merits.map((m) => (
            <div class="card p-6">
              <p class="font-medium text-fg">{m.h}</p>
              <p class="mt-2 text-pretty text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">{m.p}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-line bg-surface/30 py-16" data-reveal>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-medium">Use the plugin, not this</h2>
      <ul class="mt-5 max-w-3xl space-y-3">
        {
          [
            "Your upload comfortably carries every destination at once. If it does, the main reason to move the fan-out off your machine has already been answered.",
            "The per-destination mixes you want are ones you can lay out across OBS's six tracks. Selecting one of those per output is exactly what this plugin does, at zero cost.",
            "You want no infrastructure. A plugin that ships with your broadcast software is one fewer thing to keep alive.",
            "You are already running it and it works. There is no upgrade being sold here.",
          ].map((l) => (
            <li class="flex gap-3 text-pretty leading-relaxed text-muted">
              <span class="mt-2 h-1 w-1 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-subtle" aria-hidden="true" />
              {l}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap py-20 text-center" data-reveal>
    <h2 class="text-balance text-3xl font-medium">Still weighing it up?</h2>
    <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3">
      <a href="/comparison" class="btn btn-ghost">All five compared</a>
      <a href="/download" class="btn btn-primary">Install polyemesis</a>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-subtle">
      Also compared:
      <a href="/vs/aitum-multistream" class="text-primary underline-offset-4 hover:underline">Aitum Multistream</a>
      ·
      <a href="/vs/streamelements" class="text-primary underline-offset-4 hover:underline">StreamElements</a>
      ·
      <a href="/vs/restreamer" class="text-primary underline-offset-4 hover:underline">Restreamer and restream.io</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
